.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt SETITIMER 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm setitimer ,
.Nm getitimer ,
.Nm alarm
.Nd set and read the interval timers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/time.h
.Ft int
.Fn setitimer "int which" "const struct itimerval *new" "struct itimerval *old"
.Ft int
.Fn getitimer "int which" "struct itimerval *cur"
.In unistd.h
.Ft unsigned
.Fn alarm "unsigned seconds"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each process has three interval timers, which its threads share.
.Dv ITIMER_REAL
counts real time and posts
.Dv SIGALRM
to the process when it expires; any thread that does not block the signal
may take it.
.Dv ITIMER_VIRTUAL
counts the processor time of the process's threads in user mode and posts
.Dv SIGVTALRM ,
and
.Dv ITIMER_PROF
counts their time in user and kernel mode and posts
.Dv SIGPROF ;
each posts its signal to the thread whose tick ran it out.
Profilers sample with
.Dv ITIMER_PROF :
glibc's
.Fn profil ,
which a program built with
.Fl pg
for gprof calls, records where each
.Dv SIGPROF
found the program.
.Pp
.Fn setitimer
arms the timer
.Fa which :
.Va it_value
is the time to the first signal and
.Va it_interval
the time between each later one, or zero for a single one.
A zero
.Va it_value
disarms the timer.
The real-time timer keeps its time and interval in nanoseconds and arms
an independent local APIC event, as Linux's high-resolution timer does.
Its first
.Dv SIGALRM
never comes before the time asked for.
The timers of processor time count each tick the timer interrupt charges
to a thread of the process, in the mode it interrupted, as
.Xr times 2
counts them.
A timer of processor time starts a tick longer than its
.Va it_value ,
as Linux's adds
.Dv TICK_NSEC ,
so a 10 ms timer starts with 14 ms left and expires at the fourth tick
charged, and is armed again for its interval at the tick it runs out.
Disarming it keeps the interval it was given, which
.Fn getitimer
reports, as on Linux.
.Dv ITIMER_REAL
stops when its
.Dv SIGALRM
is posted, and a periodic one runs again when a
.Dv SIGALRM
of the process is taken, by a handler, the default action,
.Xr sigtimedwait 2
or a
.Xr signalfd 2
read, at the first time after then that is a whole number of intervals
after its previous expiry, so a late signal does not move the timer's
period, as Linux's
.Fn dequeue_signal
restarts it with
.Fn hrtimer_forward .
While its
.Dv SIGALRM
is blocked the timer posts no other, and one that is ignored and not
blocked, which is discarded, leaves the timer stopped, as on Linux.
Unless
.Fa old
is null, it receives the timer's setting before the call, as
.Fn getitimer
reports it.
.Pp
A timer event or accounting tick finds a timer run out and leaves its
signal for the next holder of the big lock, trying it at that event and
retrying a failed try after one millisecond, or at a return to user mode.
Each signal is posted once however many times its timer ran out before
the post, as Linux queues no second of a legacy signal that is pending.
A
.Fn setitimer
or
.Fn alarm
of
.Dv ITIMER_REAL
made before the post posts its
.Dv SIGALRM
first, as Linux sends it when the timer runs out.
The signal wakes the process if it is asleep in a call;
.Xr nanosleep 2 ,
.Xr poll 2
and
.Xr select 2
say when that ends their call with
.Er EINTR ,
and
.Xr sigaction 2
says it for other waits.
A call the signal interrupts is restarted after a handler with
.Dv SA_RESTART
when
.Xr sigaction 2
says it is, and otherwise fails with
.Er EINTR ;
the waits for a signal or a deadline are never restarted.
.Pp
.Fn getitimer
reports the time left before the next signal, zero when the timer is
disarmed or stopped, and the interval.
For
.Dv ITIMER_REAL
the time left is measured from its monotonic deadline and truncated to
whole microseconds.
A due callback not yet collected reports one microsecond;
a collected expiry waiting to post its signal reports zero, as on Linux.
.Pp
.Fn alarm
arms a single
.Dv SIGALRM
.Fa seconds
from now, or disarms the timer for 0.
It returns the seconds that were left before the alarm it replaces, zero
when none was armed.
musl's
.Fn alarm
calls
.Fn setitimer
and rounds any fraction of a second up; the raw call rounds to the
nearest second, and returns one when less than a second was left.
.Pp
A child of
.Xr fork 2
starts with the timers disarmed.
.Xr execve 2
keeps the timers and the pending signals, so a
.Dv SIGALRM
posted and not yet delivered, while blocked, comes to the new program
when it unblocks the signal.
.Ss Differences from Linux
The CPU-time timers count 4 ms accounting ticks.
The real-time timer reports microseconds; on a machine without a local
APIC timer and reference clock, it expires at the following tick.
The raw call refuses a null
.Fa new
with
.Er EFAULT ,
where Linux disarms the timer, and the kernel checks
.Fa which
before it reads
.Fa new .
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 104 for
.Fn setitimer ,
105 for
.Fn getitimer
and 27 for
.Fn alarm .
EBX holds
.Fa which ,
or the seconds for
.Fn alarm ;
ECX points to the new or current value and EDX to the old one.
On x86-64 the numbers are 38, 36 and 37, with the same arguments in RDI,
RSI and RDX.
The kernel's itimerval is four signed fields, of 32 bits on i386 and 64 on
x86-64: the interval's seconds and microseconds, then the value's.
Every value and interval is kept in nanoseconds and saturates at the
signed nanosecond limit, about 292 years, as Linux's ktime does.
Processor time is charged by the accounting tick, so a CPU-time timer
expires at the first tick that reaches it, and its next expiry is the
interval less the part of that tick past it.
An armed CPU-time timer with less than a microsecond left reads as one
microsecond.
A time that does not fit the structure is reported as the largest
.Vt long
of seconds and 999999 microseconds.
.Pp
musl passes that form for its 64-bit
.Vt time_t ;
before the call, its
.Fn setitimer
fails with
.Er ENOTSUP
when a seconds field does not fit in 32 bits, so its
.Fn alarm
of more than 2147483647 seconds arms nothing, leaves an alarm already
armed as it was, and returns 0.
Its
.Fn setitimer
reads
.Fa new
itself before the call, so a null or unreadable
.Fa new
faults in the caller, whatever
.Fa which
is.
It writes
.Fa old ,
when that is not null, only after the call succeeds, and its
.Fn getitimer
writes
.Fa cur
only after the call succeeds, so an unwritable
.Fa old
or
.Fa cur
faults in the caller with the call done.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn setitimer
and
.Fn getitimer
return 0 on success, or -1 on failure and set
.Va errno .
.Fn alarm
returns the seconds described above.
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The callsmusl suite arms
.Dv ITIMER_VIRTUAL
and
.Dv ITIMER_PROF
every 10 ms and requires each to post its signal three times while the
process spins.
The default suite's
.Pa tests/sigprobe.c
arms a single alarm with
.Dv SIGALRM
blocked and requires it not to end a sleep and its handler to run once
when the signal is unblocked, arms an interval timer and requires it to
end three sleeps, and requires a child of
.Xr fork 2
to have no timer.
The dhcp suite runs
.Xr arping 1
at an address nothing answers and requires its
.Fl w
deadline to end the run with status 1.
.Sh ERRORS
The kernel handlers return:
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa new
or
.Fa cur
is null or cannot be read or written, or
.Fa old
cannot be written, in which case the timer is already set.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa which
is not one of the three timers, or a time is negative or has
microseconds of a second or more.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nanosleep 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr select 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2
